  {"lesson":1,"field":"practice_questions","path":"[3].marks","expect":"1 mark each for any four of the following developed points: the cella was the god's private dwelling and worship happened in the god's presence outside; the altar was the site of animal sacrifice which required open air (smoke, fire, blood); crowds could not fit inside the restricted cella; the altar faced east so the sacrificing priest could look towards the rising sun; the god's statue stood inside as the permanent resident while the community gathered outside; Roman temples on a podium with frontal steps meant the altar directly faced the entrance steps.","value":"1 mark each for any four of the following developed points: the cella was the god's private dwelling and worship happened in the god's presence outside; the altar was the site of animal sacrifice which required open air (smoke, fire, blood); crowds could not fit inside the restricted cella; the god's statue stood inside as the permanent resident while the community gathered outside; Roman temples on a podium with frontal steps meant the altar directly faced the entrance steps.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F3: eastward altar orientation was not universal."},
  {"lesson":1,"field":"flashcard_questions","path":"[1].a","expect":"Outside the temple, to the east, in front of the entrance.","value":"Outside the temple, in front of the entrance.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F4: eastward altar orientation was not universal."},
  {"lesson":2,"field":"content_html","find":"A fulfilled promise to dedicate an offering was called a <em>vow</em> in Latin;","replace":"A promise to dedicate an offering was a <em>votum</em> in Latin;","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F5: votum is the Latin term."},
  {"lesson":2,"field":"content_html","find":"If the priest made a mistake &mdash; mispronounced a word, broke the silence, or the animal escaped &mdash; the entire sacrifice had to be repeated from the start. This principle of <em>piacula</em> (atonement) meant that religious officials were extremely careful and that the procedures were highly formalised compared to Greek practice.","replace":"If a priest made a mistake, a further sacrifice could be needed to make amends. A <em>piaculum</em> was an expiatory sacrifice, and Roman procedures were highly formalised.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F6: piaculum is an expiatory sacrifice; a full restart was not the universal consequence."},
  {"lesson":2,"field":"conclusion_html","find":"Roman sacrifice placed greater emphasis on ritual precision (the <em>haruspex</em>, correct formulae, absolute silence); any error required the whole ceremony to be repeated from the beginning.","replace":"Roman sacrifice placed great emphasis on correct ritual (the <em>haruspex</em>, correct formulae and silence); an error could require an expiatory sacrifice.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F7: an error could require expiation, not invariably full repetition."},
  {"lesson":2,"field":"practice_questions","path":"[5].marks","expect":"Mastering (5-6): Two clearly explained differences (e.g. Roman priest covered head capite velato vs Greek bare-headed; Roman haruspex examined entrails formally vs Greek more informal; Roman demand for ritual perfection and repetition if error vs Greek less rigid; suovetaurilia in Rome vs more varied animals in Greece) each with a named example and a clear sense of what the difference reveals about each culture's attitudes to the gods.\nSecure (3-4): Two differences explained with some evidence.\nDeveloping (1-2): One difference stated or two superficially mentioned.","value":"Mastering (5-6): Two clearly explained differences (e.g. Roman priest covered head capite velato vs Greek bare-headed; Roman haruspex examined entrails formally vs Greek more informal; suovetaurilia in Rome vs more varied animals in Greece) each with a named example and a clear sense of what the difference reveals about each culture's attitudes to the gods.\nSecure (3-4): Two differences explained with some evidence.\nDeveloping (1-2): One difference stated or two superficially mentioned.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F8: removes the inaccurate universal-repetition contrast."},
  {"lesson":2,"field":"flashcard_questions","path":"[10].q","expect":"Why did Roman ritual have to be repeated from the beginning if an error was made?","value":"What could happen if an error was made in Roman ritual?","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F9: repetition from the beginning was not universal."},
  {"lesson":2,"field":"flashcard_questions","path":"[10].a","expect":"Romans required absolute ritual correctness; any mistake invalidated the ceremony.","value":"An expiatory sacrifice could be offered to make amends for the error.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F10: gives the verified consequence of a ritual error."},
  {"lesson":3,"field":"content_html","find":"A Greek male priest (hiereus) or female priestess (hiereia) responsible for the maintenance of a temple and the performance of its rituals.","replace":"A Greek male priest responsible for the maintenance of a temple and the performance of its rituals.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F11: hiereus is male; hiereia is the female term."},
  {"lesson":3,"field":"glossary_terms","path":"[0].definition","expect":"A Greek male priest (hiereus) or female priestess (hiereia) responsible for the maintenance of a temple and the performance of its rituals.","value":"A Greek male priest responsible for the maintenance of a temple and the performance of its rituals.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F12: hiereus is male; hiereia is the female term."},
  {"lesson":3,"field":"content_html","find":"An eagle flying to the right was a good omen; a raven on the left was bad.","replace":"The meaning of a bird sign depended on its species, behaviour, direction and the circumstances.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F13: Roman augury did not use those fixed rules."},
  {"lesson":5,"field":"content_html","find":"Romans used both burial and cremation, with cremation becoming fashionable among the elite during the Imperial period before inhumation (burial) returned to favour in the 2nd and 3rd centuries AD, partly under the influence of Eastern religions and later Christianity.","replace":"Romans used both burial and cremation. Cremation was common from the later Republic into the early Empire, then inhumation gradually became more common. The reasons for this change are uncertain.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F14: corrects the chronology and removes an unsupported causal claim."},
  {"lesson":5,"field":"practice_questions","path":"[4].marks","expect":"Mastering (9-10): Sustained balanced argument identifying genuine similarities (both required burial outside city walls; both had ritual laying-out of the body; both used grave goods; both had festivals for the dead; both placed coins for Charon) alongside real differences (Roman funeral clubs for the poor with no Greek equivalent; Roman use of ancestor masks in processions; Lemuria specifically for malevolent spirits vs Genesia for honoured ancestors; Roman cremation was an elite fashion linked to Imperial period; stelai in Greece vs elaborate roadside tombs and mausoleums in Rome); evidence from named sources (Sophocles' Antigone, the Hegeso Stele, Parentalia and Lemuria); clear judgement.\nSecure (6-8): Clear argument with evidence for similarities and differences and a conclusion.\nDeveloping (3-5): Points about one side with limited comparison or source use.\nEmerging (1-2): Descriptive account; little argument.","value":"Mastering (9-10): Sustained balanced argument identifying genuine similarities (both required burial outside city walls; both had ritual laying-out of the body; both used grave goods; both had festivals for the dead; both placed coins for Charon) alongside real differences (Roman funeral clubs for the poor with no Greek equivalent; Roman use of ancestor masks in processions; Lemuria specifically for malevolent spirits vs Genesia for honoured ancestors; Roman cremation was common before inhumation became more common; stelai in Greece vs elaborate roadside tombs and mausoleums in Rome); evidence from named sources (Sophocles' Antigone, the Hegeso Stele, Parentalia and Lemuria); clear judgement.\nSecure (6-8): Clear argument with evidence for similarities and differences and a conclusion.\nDeveloping (3-5): Points about one side with limited comparison or source use.\nEmerging (1-2): Descriptive account; little argument.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F15: corrects the cremation chronology in the answer guidance."}
