+  {"lesson":3,"field":"content_html","find":"An eagle flying to the right was a good omen; a raven on the left was bad.","replace":"The meaning of a bird sign depended on its species, behaviour, direction and the circumstances.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F13: Roman augury did not use those fixed rules."},
+  {"lesson":5,"field":"content_html","find":"Romans used both burial and cremation, with cremation becoming fashionable among the elite during the Imperial period before inhumation (burial) returned to favour in the 2nd and 3rd centuries AD, partly under the influence of Eastern religions and later Christianity.","replace":"Romans used both burial and cremation. Cremation was common from the later Republic into the early Empire, then inhumation gradually became more common. The reasons for this change are uncertain.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F14: corrects the chronology and removes an unsupported causal claim."},
+  {"lesson":5,"field":"practice_questions","path":"[4].marks","expect":"Mastering (9-10): Sustained balanced argument identifying genuine similarities (both required burial outside city walls; both had ritual laying-out of the body; both used grave goods; both had festivals for the dead; both placed coins for Charon) alongside real differences (Roman funeral clubs for the poor with no Greek equivalent; Roman use of ancestor masks in processions; Lemuria specifically for malevolent spirits vs Genesia for honoured ancestors; Roman cremation was an elite fashion linked to Imperial period; stelai in Greece vs elaborate roadside tombs and mausoleums in Rome); evidence from named sources (Sophocles' Antigone, the Hegeso Stele, Parentalia and Lemuria); clear judgement.\nSecure (6-8): Clear argument with evidence for similarities and differences and a conclusion.\nDeveloping (3-5): Points about one side with limited comparison or source use.\nEmerging (1-2): Descriptive account; little argument.","value":"Mastering (9-10): Sustained balanced argument identifying genuine similarities (both required burial outside city walls; both had ritual laying-out of the body; both used grave goods; both had festivals for the dead; both placed coins for Charon) alongside real differences (Roman funeral clubs for the poor with no Greek equivalent; Roman use of ancestor masks in processions; Lemuria specifically for malevolent spirits vs Genesia for honoured ancestors; Roman cremation was common before inhumation became more common; stelai in Greece vs elaborate roadside tombs and mausoleums in Rome); evidence from named sources (Sophocles' Antigone, the Hegeso Stele, Parentalia and Lemuria); clear judgement.\nSecure (6-8): Clear argument with evidence for similarities and differences and a conclusion.\nDeveloping (3-5): Points about one side with limited comparison or source use.\nEmerging (1-2): Descriptive account; little argument.","severity":"MEDIUM","note":"F15: corrects the cremation chronology in the answer guidance."}
