+{"unit":"greek-and-roman-religion","checked_at":"2026-09-05T19:38:01.0446786+01:00","spec_used":"specs/ocr/classical-civilisation-J199.md","findings":[{"id":"F1","lesson":1,"field":"content_html","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"the east-facing entrance","truth":"Greek temple entrances were usually east-facing, but there were exceptions; the direction is not universal.","evidence":"Ancient Greek temple (fetched), Function and design: named west- and north–south-facing exceptions.","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F2","lesson":1,"field":"content_html","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"The altar stood outside the temple entirely, always to the east.","truth":"The altar was outside the temple; it was not always to the east.","evidence":"Ancient Greek temple (fetched), Function and design: temple orientations had named exceptions.","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F3","lesson":1,"field":"practice_questions","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"the altar faced east so the sacrificing priest could look towards the rising sun","truth":"An altar’s eastward position was not universal, so it cannot be a required feature of an answer.","evidence":"Ancient Greek temple (fetched), Function and design: named west- and north–south-facing exceptions.","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F4","lesson":1,"field":"flashcard_questions","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"Outside the temple, to the east, in front of the entrance.","truth":"The altar was outside the temple, in front of the entrance; eastward orientation was usual, not universal.","evidence":"Ancient Greek temple (fetched), Function and design: named west- and north–south-facing exceptions.","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F5","lesson":2,"field":"content_html","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"A fulfilled promise to dedicate an offering was called a vow in Latin","truth":"The Latin term is votum; it means a vow or promise and can also denote what fulfils it.","evidence":"Votum (fetched): defines votum and its meanings in Roman religion.","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F6","lesson":2,"field":"content_html","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"This principle of piacula (atonement) meant that religious officials were extremely careful","truth":"Piaculum is singular and means an expiatory sacrifice or victim; it is not the name of a principle of ritual correctness.","evidence":"Glossary of ancient Roman religion (fetched), piaculum entry; it defines a piaculum as an expiatory sacrifice.","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F7","lesson":2,"field":"conclusion_html","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"any error required the whole ceremony to be repeated from the beginning","truth":"A ritual error could require an expiatory sacrifice; a universal restart rule is too absolute.","evidence":"Glossary of ancient Roman religion (fetched): a hostia succidanea followed a failed sacrifice; piaculum was expiatory.","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F8","lesson":2,"field":"practice_questions","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"Roman demand for ritual perfection and repetition if error","truth":"Roman ritual stressed correct performance, but an error could be addressed with an expiatory sacrifice rather than a universal restart rule.","evidence":"Glossary of ancient Roman religion (fetched), piaculum and hostia succidanea entries.","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F9","lesson":2,"field":"flashcard_questions","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"Roman ritual had to be repeated from the beginning if an error was made","truth":"An error could require an expiatory sacrifice; the consequence was not invariably a full repetition.","evidence":"Glossary of ancient Roman religion (fetched), piaculum and hostia succidanea entries.","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F10","lesson":2,"field":"flashcard_questions","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"Romans required absolute ritual correctness; any mistake invalidated the ceremony.","truth":"Roman ritual stressed correct performance, and an error could require an expiatory sacrifice.","evidence":"Glossary of ancient Roman religion (fetched), piaculum and hostia succidanea entries.","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F11","lesson":3,"field":"content_html","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"A Greek male priest (hiereus) or female priestess (hiereia)","truth":"Hiereus is the male priest; hiereia is the female priestess.","evidence":"OCR specification, Myth and Religion key topics: ‘hiereus and hiereia (priests and priestesses)’.","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F12","lesson":3,"field":"glossary_terms","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"A Greek male priest (hiereus) or female priestess (hiereia)","truth":"Hiereus is the male priest; hiereia is the female priestess.","evidence":"OCR specification, Myth and Religion key topics: ‘hiereus and hiereia (priests and priestesses)’.","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F13","lesson":3,"field":"content_html","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"An eagle flying to the right was a good omen; a raven on the left was bad.","truth":"The meaning of a bird sign depended on species, behaviour, direction and circumstances; those fixed rules are inaccurate.","evidence":"Augury (fetched), Types of auspices: meanings varied by bird, side and circumstances.","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F14","lesson":5,"field":"content_html","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"cremation becoming fashionable among the elite during the Imperial period before inhumation returned to favour in the 2nd and 3rd centuries AD, partly under the influence of Eastern religions and later Christianity","truth":"Cremation was common from the later Republic into the early Empire; inhumation later became more common, and the causes remain uncertain.","evidence":"Roman funerary practices (fetched), Cremation, inhumation and os resectum: chronology and causes are described as uncertain.","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F15","lesson":5,"field":"practice_questions","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"Roman cremation was an elite fashion linked to Imperial period","truth":"Roman cremation was common before inhumation became more common; it was not merely an Imperial elite fashion.","evidence":"Roman funerary practices (fetched), Cremation, inhumation and os resectum.","verdict":"FIX"},{"id":"F16","lesson":3,"field":"content_html","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"Her often ambiguous or cryptic utterances were then transmitted by the priests in verse.","truth":"Either frame this as one ancient tradition, or remove it: ancient accounts conflict, and whether priests interpreted or versified the Pythia’s words is disputed.","evidence":"Pythia (fetched), Oracle procedure: the role of the prophetai is unclear and proposed interpretations are contested.","verdict":"ADJUDICATE"},{"id":"F17","lesson":4,"field":"content_html","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"the Parthenon frieze ... depicts the Panathenaic procession","truth":"Retain the conventional identification but label it an interpretation, or teach the rival Erechtheus interpretation; neither should be presented as settled fact.","evidence":"Parthenon (fetched), Function: Connelly’s alternative reading is supported by some scholars and disputed by others.","verdict":"ADJUDICATE"},{"id":"F18","lesson":5,"field":"content_html","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"placing a coin in the mouth or on the eyes of the deceased ... confirms the literary accounts","truth":"A coin custom is attested but was not universal; evidence supports placement in or on the mouth, not the eyes. Correcting the linked conclusion, flashcard, glossary and checks would require a coordinated rewrite.","evidence":"Charon’s obol (fetched): practice was neither pervasive nor restricted to a coin in the mouth; notes reject a classical coin-on-eye custom. Roman funerary practices (fetched) says it was not consistent everywhere.","verdict":"ADJUDICATE"},{"id":"F19","lesson":5,"field":"content_html","severity":"MEDIUM","claim":"The Genesia was an Athenian public festival" ,"truth":"The lesson also calls Genesia ‘primarily a family affair’ in its practice answer. Resolve the public-versus-familial character from a scholarly source, then make the field, glossary and answer key consistent.","evidence":"Raw L05 contains both claims; OCR specification lists Genesia but does not define its public or private character. The fetched Genesia page provided no substantive article.","verdict":"ADJUDICATE"}],"lessons_clean":[],"summary":"Five lessons checked against the OCR specification and fetched sources. Fifteen surgical factual corrections are supplied; four source-interpretation or multi-field issues require adjudication rather than edits."}

